AB  - Aerospace control software is a core component of space missions and has been increasingly studied in the context of AI-driven software synthesis to improve development efficiency and quality. However, existing approaches suffer from a fundamental semantic gap between software requirements and programs, which limits their ability to accurately synthesize software structure and behavior. Introducing an intermediate software architecture is a common strategy to mitigate this gap, yet current architectural models remain insufficient for expressing multi-level data interactions, branching control logic, and cross-cycle dynamic state transitions in aerospace control software, thereby constraining synthesis accuracy. To overcome these challenges, this paper proposes CSAML, a novel control software architecture modeling language with rigorously formalized syntax and semantics. CSAML precisely captures data flows, control logic, and state transition behaviors in aerospace systems. In addition, this paper introduces a pattern-based refinement mechanism that automatically identifies and recommends reusable architectural patterns to express fine-grained structures and behaviors. For correctness assurance, transformation rules are developed to translate CSAML models into Promela, enabling property specification and formal validation using the SPIN model checker. A case study on a sun-search satellite control system demonstrates the effectiveness of CSAML in modeling data flows, control logic, and state transitions, establishing a formal foundation for verifiable, intelligent synthesis of safety-critical aerospace control software architectures.
