AB  - Fault injection techniques are broadly used for dependability analysis of cyber–physical systems; however, they need to be optimized in terms of fault coverage and performance due to the enormous fault space. In this paper, we provide a systematic literature review of existing methods for model-implemented fault injection. Our main research questions investigate (1) the goals of performing model-implemented fault injection, (2) the existing solutions to optimize model-implemented fault injection, (3) identifying fault model and parameters, (4) the metrics that are being used and (5) the trends and potential research gaps. We investigated 1633 papers and identified 90 primary relevant studies. Then, we systematically summarized and categorized their fault injection goals, optimization methods, fault models, evaluation methods, and metrics. We also explore the relationship between the method, the metrics, and the fault model. Finally, we identified the trend and potential research gaps in this domain. We found that fault injection techniques are mostly used to measure dependability attributes, and the authors utilize machine learning and system simplification to optimize fault simulation. Additionally, most studies focus on the pre-injection and execution phases of fault injection. Therefore, there is a need for post-injection analysis of results. We believe that practitioners and researchers can use the results of this study to address the identified research gaps.
ER  - 

